  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nAdd an optional, source-pinned adapter for the historical h=10/h=12 mixed-point frame audits. It replaces only the running process's rational-subspace inclusion predicate with exact integer annihilator equations. All original construction, Gram, complement, frame-nesting and endpoint checks remain active.\n\nStatus: verification tooling. No new \u03ba. The selected conditional \u03ba remains 0.001672660197076918. No existing source, selected certificate, frozen evidence or upstream/ file changes.\n\nFor a canonical target basis with rows v_i, pivot columns p_i and positive pivots c_i, let L = lcm(c_i). A source row u belongs to its rational span exactly when every nonpivot coordinate j satisfies:\n\n    L*u[j] - sum_i u[p_i]*(L/c_i)*v_i[j] = 0.\n\nThe kernel preserves the reference predicate's rejection of noncanonical target presentations. It validates immutable rectangular integer inputs before consulting a bounded 8,192-entry cache. No whole-audit verdict is cached.\n\n## Engineering result\n\nFive alternating fresh-process pairs on Linux x86_64, CPython 3.12.14:\n\n- Median complete-process wall time: 2.798839 \u2192 2.520628 seconds.\n- Ratio: 1.110\u00d7, or 9.94% less time.\n- Median peak RSS: 89,128 \u2192 73,748 KiB, or 17.26% less.\n- All ten complete mathematical payloads agree exactly.\n\nEvery sample is retained in BENCHMARK.json. Application caches start empty; filesystem caches were not flushed. No samples were discarded and no timing threshold determines correctness. A separate validation pair measured 1.033\u00d7, illustrating runtime variation.\n\nThese measurements concern this historical verifier only. They establish neither a selected-supplier speedup nor a multiplication-runtime improvement.\n\n## Task, provenance and overlap\n\nOS-random assignment seed: 88, verification engineering. The replay-runtime card is refined to one historical inclusion-check bottleneck. TASK.json records three candidate tasks, selection, stopping rule and the local claim/result.\n\nExact starting commit:\n410026dc5513b87db92ae98571d1d5984c69a983\n\nAll six live dependencies are retained unchanged at that commit and pinned by Git blob and SHA256 in SOURCES.json:\n\n- scripts/rational_span_frames.py\n- scripts/experiments/mixed_point_circuit.py\n- scripts/exclusion_circuit.py\n- scripts/paired_exclusion_circuit.py\n- scripts/certify.py\n- tests/benchmark_rational_span_frames.py\n\nNo third-party libraries are required. The registered replay uses Python 3.12 on Linux/macOS.\n\nPredecessor and concurrent work:\n\n- Richard Hoekstra's PR239, fd687eecd781631bc38ae8b03a83d00c13f05aa7, supplied the incorporated dual-Gram optimization and unchanged customer benchmark.\n- Richard Hoekstra's pending PR1234, 8812ca21c43f5cea16276c7fa539fa8d1559b0f1, optimizes normalization and complements on the same customer. Its actual Git diff was inspected. This contribution instead changes inclusion; PR1234 is not imported and no combined speedup is claimed.\n- Aman's PR367, 522285db971642c2d744cc8d45f2a5ade3a55e19, accelerates rank/canonical-basis calculations on a different PR364 workload. No code is imported from it.\n\nLinks:\nhttps://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/239\nhttps://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1234\nhttps://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/367\n\nBefore publication, Git confirmed unchanged main and PR1234 heads. Focused public PR searches, discussions, coordination comments, the review ledger and local negative results were checked. This is scoped overlap coverage, not an exhaustive novelty certificate. API posting was unavailable, so coordination records remain local.\n\n## Reproduction and validation\n\nRecommended command from the repository root:\n\n    python3 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/rational-containment-performance\n\nExpected final messages:\n\n    PASS_RATIONAL_CONTAINMENT calls=42860 kernel_mutants=4 source_mutants=6 frame_mutants=2 adapter_controls=2\n    PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/rational-containment-performance outcome=completed\n\nAllow approximately 25 seconds on the measured host. The command requires full Git provenance, performs no network access, and includes one fresh-process timing pair.\n\nCompleted checks:\n\n- All 42,860 live inclusion decisions across 7,614 distinct targets agree.\n- Complete reference, accelerated and comparison-mode audit payloads agree.\n- Eight independent kernel test methods pass normally and under optimized Python.\n- Exhaustive coverage includes 5,460 coordinate-subspace pairs and 4,096 binary 2\u00d73 presentation pairs, plus 288 seed-88 signed cases.\n- Independent Fraction elimination checks arithmetic; named cases cover non-unit and 127-bit pivots, empty/dependent/noncanonical spaces, eviction and malformed inputs.\n- Four faulty kernels produce arithmetic test failures, with no execution errors.\n- Six source mutations reject.\n- Two actual compiled-frame mutations reject with identical errors in both engines.\n- Existing-output and disabled-assertion adapter controls reject.\n- Registered replay, metadata/Git/SHA256 pins, selected-record verification, syntax and staged whitespace checks pass.\n\nComplete payload SHA256:\nfaa0f9c0159a6e70d4e2cfcdb4012ea62ef350bd26b81312d11da4a770f8b218\n\nOrdered inclusion transcript SHA256:\n26d55e53c7e9f7ae83b3f3d3c4e9c91735e57f11a1f0db54bb02e895bc867157\n\n## Limitations and remaining obligations\n\nPerformance coverage is limited to the pinned historical h=10/h=12 workload. Input equivalence concerns rectangular integer matrices of common width. The process adapter is sequential and remains opt-in.\n\nFull make verify, frontier1094 cold replay, Lean/native builds and remote CI were not run. Finite checks do not constitute formal verification of the multiplication theorem. Inherited all-size/compiler, common-label/mutable-source realization, hosting, precision/tape, analytic and bit-interface assumptions remain.\n\n## Credit\n\nPrepared for @CubeJerry with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance in analysis, implementation, experiments, verification and packaging. Credit Douglas Colkitt's historical construction/frame semantics, Richard Hoekstra's PR239 and concurrent PR1234, and Aman's separate PR367. No independent human review is claimed.\n\nNew material is Apache-2.0. Predecessor files and notices remain unchanged.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nPin a reviewed PR1234 implementation and repeat complete decision equivalence and paired measurements with both changes. This would establish whether their savings compose; the current measurements do not answer that question. Direct activation also requires maintainer review and applicable provenance updates.",
